// error
// #2236: SPEC §17.4 says "`main` takes no parameters", and nothing enforced it.
// This exact spelling is the reflex of anyone arriving from C, Java, Go or Rust,
// and it is the WORST of the shapes because it does not crash: measured on brew
// 0.1.8 and on this tree before the rule, `function main(a: int)` printed
// `a=4363964576` and exited **0**. The parameter is read out of a register the
// entry trampoline never wrote, so a program looking for its command-line
// arguments silently got a stack address and reported success.
//
// It compounds with #2124 (`bit run` discards argv): a developer hunting for
// arguments tries the parameter spelling first, which is why the hint names
// `std/os` rather than only listing the legal forms.
//
// The two-parameter shape (SIGSEGV) and the result-type rule live in
// check_main_signature.bit; the float register hole in
// check_main_result_float.bit. What must STAY legal — all four §17.4 forms, end
// to end with their exit codes and stderr — is pinned by tests/bit/mainexit.bit,
// not here.
function main(args: []string) {
  print("argc=")
  print("${len(args)}")
  print("\n")
}
